<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hilton of Cadboll Stone</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 800</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hilton of Cadboll Stone is a Pictish cross-slab of about 800 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-ross">Easter Ross</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. Its surviving face shows a hunting scene with a woman riding side-saddle, trumpeters and animals, framed by vine-scroll and Pictish symbols; the cross on the other side was cut away in 1676 for a memorial. The upper part is in the National Museum of Scotland, and the lower part was excavated at the original site in 2001.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hilton-of-cadboll-stone.jpg" alt="Hilton of Cadboll Stone" width="320" height="598" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Catfish Jim and the soapdish · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hilton_of_Cadboll_nms.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
